+# PlacementRules spec cleanup
+
+`PlacementRules.spec.luau` drops from 31 tests to 19. Every test now goes through the public
+`snap` and `validate` functions, so a refactor that keeps placement working won't turn it red.
+`PlacementRules.luau` is unchanged.
+
+## The two failing tests
+
+- **"rejects a position that isn't a Vector3"** called its matchers with a colon
+  (`expect(ok):toBe(false)`). Jest Roblox matchers are called with `.`. The colon passes the
+  matcher object in as the expected value, so the test could never pass. That case is now a row
+  in the rejection table.
+- **"doesn't mark the cell occupied"** asserted `expect(count).toBeFalsy()` with `count == 0`.
+  In Luau, `0` is truthy, so the test always failed. It is now "leaves occupied unchanged when it
+  accepts", which asserts `occupied` still equals `{}` and also checks the call was accepted.
+  Before, a rejection would also have passed.
+
+## Removed
+
+| Test | Why |
+|---|---|
+| `snap` "snaps positions to cell centers" | Worked out the expected value with the same `floor` formula the code uses, so it could never disagree with the code. |
+| `snap` 6-row table | Several rows covered the same case, and the literals assumed `BlockSize = 4`. Replaced by a 4-row table (below). |
+| `_isFinite` (3 tests) | Tested a private helper. NaN and infinity are now rows in `validate`'s rejection table. The `_isFinite` export is still in the module because this change doesn't touch it. Nothing uses it now, so a later change can remove it. |
+| "calls snap exactly once" | Counted calls to an internal function. Inlining `snap` would break it even though placement still works. |
+| "returns the snapped center" | Got its expected value by calling `snap`. The acceptance table now checks the returned center against independently computed values. |
+| One test per block type (5 tests built at runtime) | Merged into one test that loops over `BlockTypes.List` and lists any type that gets rejected. |
+| "rejects placing twice in the same cell" | Repeated "rejects a cell that is already occupied". It also only passed because the earlier test had left the shared `occupied` table filled in. |
+| Height table (4 rows), reach table (3 rows) | Hard-coded `MaxHeight = 200`, `MaxReach = 24` and `BlockSize = 4`. Their boundary rows moved into the accept and reject tables, using values computed from the module constants. |
+| `BlockTypes` "has the right block types" | Copied catalog colors, materials and transparency field by field. Those are presentation, and they belong to `BlockTypes`, not `PlacementRules`. |
+
+The module-level `occupied` table that several tests shared is gone, so each test now builds its
+own state.
+
+## What the suite covers now
+
+Each rule documented in `PlacementRules.luau`, and the test that covers it:
+
+- **snap** (4 rows): a cell's lower corner is in that cell, the cell resting on the Baseplate has
+  its center at `BlockSize / 2`, a point inside a cell snaps to its center, a cell's upper edge
+  belongs to the next cell, and negative positions snap away from zero. Each row uses different
+  values on X, Y and Z, so swapping two axes would fail.
+- **validate accepts** (3 rows): the cell resting on the Baseplate, the highest cell at or below
+  `MaxHeight`, and a cell exactly `MaxReach` away. Each row passes a position that is off the
+  cell's center and checks that the returned value is the center.
+- **validate accepts every block type** (1 test, listing any type that gets rejected).
+- **validate rejects** (10 rows, each changing one thing in an otherwise valid request): unknown
+  block type name, a name that isn't a string, a position that isn't a Vector3, NaN, infinity, a
+  cell below the Baseplate, a cell above `MaxHeight`, no character (`rootPosition == nil`), a cell
+  just past `MaxReach`, and an occupied cell. **The no-character rule had no test before.**
+- **validate never changes `occupied`** (1 test).
+
+## Assumptions
+
+- **Reason strings are not asserted.** The module documents them as "a short reason for the server
+  log", so their wording isn't something callers rely on. Each rejection row changes only one
+  thing in a valid request, which keeps a row from passing because a different rule rejected it.
+- **Sizes and limits come from the modules.** `BlockSize`, `MaxHeight` and `MaxReach` are read
+  from `BlockTypes` and `PlacementRules`, so changing those numbers doesn't break the spec. The
+  valid request uses `BlockTypes.List[1]`, so renaming a block type doesn't break it either.
+- **The spec uses the style guide's form**: `--!strict`, `const`, and requires by
+  `@game/...` string paths instead of `game:GetService` paths.
+
+## Verification
+
+`luau-lsp analyze`, `selene src` and `stylua --check src` all pass. Specs can't run in this
+environment, so **none of the 19 tests have been run**. Before merging, run
+`Specs/PlacementRules.spec` in Studio, or let CI run it.
